{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>I said I would end them.</b> I said in my heart: I will put an end to them. And one may also explain <b>אַפְאֶיהֶם</b> as: I will set them as a פֵּאָה — to cast them off from Me as ownerless. Its parallel we find in Ezra (Nehemiah 9): ״וַתִּתֵּן לָהֶם מַמְלָכוֹת וַעֲמָמִים וַתַּחְלְקֵם לְפֵאָה״ — as ownerless; and so Menachem explained it. And there are those who interpret it as the Targum renders: ״יָחוּל רוּגְזִי עֲלֵיהוֹן״ — but this is untenable, for if so it should have been written אֲאַפְאֵיהֶם — one alef for the particle of use and one for the root, like ״אֲאַזֶּרְךָ״ (Isaiah 45), ״אֲאַמִּיצְכֶם בְּמוֹ פִי״ (Job 15), and the middle alef is not appropriate in it at all. And Onkelos translated according to the language of the beraita found in Sifrei, which divides this word into three words: אָמַרְתִּי אַף אֵי הֵם — I said: In My wrath I will make them as though they are not, so that those who see them would say of them: Where are they?"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:23.894197050Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}